findings.description:
Found by generalising NR-103's root cause: a method whose name asks a question and whose body can only answer yes. An AST sweep over app/ found 13 such functions.

    def validate_code(self, system: str, code: str) -> bool:
        return True

and beside it

    def lookup_code(self, system: str, code: str) -> dict:
        return {"system": system, "code": code, "display": code}

so an unrecognised code is not merely accepted, it is handed back with itself as its human-readable display name. For SNOMED, LOINC or ICD that turns an arbitrary string into something that renders like a validated clinical concept.

THREE LAYERS OF UNREACHABLE, all measured rather than assumed: validate_code has zero call sites anywhere in backend/; FHIRTerminologyService is exported from app/national/fhir/__init__.py and re-exported from app/fhir/__init__.py but never instantiated; and ref_fhir_terminology — the model that would hold the real code system, complete with a unique index on (system, concept_code) — is NOT among the 422 built physical tables.

That is why this is P3 and not the P2 that NR-102 got. NR-102's endpoint was served and answerable by any authenticated caller. This one cannot be reached at all. It is registered because it is a trap: the day someone wires terminology validation, it passes everything silently, and nothing would fail.

The other ten hits from the same sweep are app/capabilities/*/capability.py validate() stubs, which return True alongside an execute() that returns a hard-coded {'success': True}. They are generated scaffolding and already covered by NR-050 and NR-053; they are not re-registered here.
